WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.779
This is the executive
briefing for Book 01 of the

3
00:00:03.779 --> 00:00:07.400
Federal Modernization Workbook,
The Transformation Arc.

4
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:11.499
It runs about thirty minutes, and it's
for the people who sponsor, fund,

5
00:00:11.499 --> 00:00:16.111
secure and authorize the move from a
domain-joined estate to cloud identity,

6
00:00:16.111 --> 00:00:19.625
cloud device management and
governed hybrid operations.

7
00:00:19.625 --> 00:00:22.337
The argument fits in three sentences.

8
00:00:22.337 --> 00:00:27.352
For twenty years Active Directory
quietly did governance nobody wrote down,

9
00:00:27.352 --> 00:00:31.349
so modernization is a governance
rebuild, not a platform swap.

10
00:00:31.349 --> 00:00:34.651
The estate has to be analyzed
before anything moves,

11
00:00:34.651 --> 00:00:38.950
and the work delivered in gated
phases with a rollback at every step.

12
00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:43.512
And leadership's job is to conduct the
orchestra, not to play the instruments.

13
00:00:43.512 --> 00:00:46.686
I'll cover why this is hard,
what changes, who leads,

14
00:00:46.686 --> 00:00:51.288
and how to deliver it, all the way to
the retirement of Active Directory.

15
00:00:51.288 --> 00:00:53.775
There's no configuration on these slides.

16
00:00:53.775 --> 00:00:58.400
Each decision slide names the decision,
who owns it, and when it's needed.

17
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.900
Let's start with how the estate got here.

18
00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.963
Federal IT reached its
current shape in four eras,

19
00:01:07.963 --> 00:01:10.225
and each one solved a real problem.

20
00:01:10.225 --> 00:01:15.700
The mainframe held the data and enforced
access to it: one truth, in one box.

21
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:20.035
Distributed systems put a database
and a directory in every region,

22
00:01:20.035 --> 00:01:22.512
and nobody replaced that single truth.

23
00:01:22.512 --> 00:01:25.213
Then email and workloads
moved to the cloud

24
00:01:25.213 --> 00:01:27.913
while identity stayed in regional domains.

25
00:01:27.913 --> 00:01:30.891
Now the same person can
appear as several records,

26
00:01:30.891 --> 00:01:33.362
and no one can say which is authoritative.

27
00:01:33.362 --> 00:01:35.362
That's why modernization stalls.

28
00:01:35.362 --> 00:01:38.262
Each step assumes the one
before it is finished.

29
00:01:38.262 --> 00:01:43.110
Zero Trust assumes authoritative cloud
identity, which assumes a clean directory,

30
00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:46.763
which assumes governed groups,
which assume someone owns them.

31
00:01:46.763 --> 00:01:49.763
That last step is where
many agencies are stuck.

32
00:01:49.763 --> 00:01:52.310
Meanwhile the mandates arrive with dates,

33
00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:55.513
and each assumes a foundation
the estate never built.

34
00:01:55.513 --> 00:01:59.151
So the first decision is about
ownership, not technology.

35
00:01:59.151 --> 00:02:02.562
Before anything is planned,
name the executive sponsor,

36
00:02:02.562 --> 00:02:06.863
the steward who runs the program
office, and the authorizing official.

37
00:02:06.863 --> 00:02:10.251
The roadmap makes that the entry
condition for its first phase.

38
00:02:10.251 --> 00:02:13.230
Next, the problem in precise terms.

39
00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.592
Active Directory shipped with Windows
2000 as an identity directory.

40
00:02:21.592 --> 00:02:25.961
Over twenty-five years every Windows-era
service wired itself into it,

41
00:02:25.961 --> 00:02:30.104
because it was the only governed,
replicated namespace available.

42
00:02:30.104 --> 00:02:32.643
Nobody wrote down how much
governance it carried.

43
00:02:32.643 --> 00:02:36.918
The organizational unit (OU)
tree wasn't a folder system.

44
00:02:36.918 --> 00:02:41.316
Placing one user in a branch set their
administrators, their Group Policy,

45
00:02:41.316 --> 00:02:44.705
their groups, their delegation
and their audit boundary.

46
00:02:44.705 --> 00:02:46.592
One act, five effects.

47
00:02:46.592 --> 00:02:52.005
Entra ID has no OU tree, so each
effect has to be rebuilt on purpose.

48
00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:56.992
Most migration plans count users,
computers, groups and Group Policy.

49
00:02:56.992 --> 00:03:02.783
The program counts eleven categories,
adding service accounts, DNS and DHCP,

50
00:03:02.783 --> 00:03:07.930
certificate services, RADIUS,
LDAP-bound applications and trusts.

51
00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:09.368
Those fail silently.

52
00:03:09.368 --> 00:03:11.843
No log line says governance has failed.

53
00:03:11.843 --> 00:03:14.941
Microsoft hasn't deprecated
Active Directory,

54
00:03:14.941 --> 00:03:19.231
so there's no vendor deadline;
retiring it is the agency's decision.

55
00:03:19.231 --> 00:03:22.678
That makes scope a decision
for the sponsor and the CIO,

56
00:03:22.678 --> 00:03:27.274
when scope and funding are set: fund
this as a governance transformation

57
00:03:27.274 --> 00:03:31.105
across all eleven categories,
not a users-and-groups project.

58
00:03:31.105 --> 00:03:34.580
Next, what has to be true
for the target to hold.

59
00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:43.093
The workbook reads federal Zero Trust
guidance as requiring five things.

60
00:03:43.093 --> 00:03:47.555
No single document publishes this
list; it's the program's synthesis.

61
00:03:47.555 --> 00:03:49.843
One authoritative source of identity.

62
00:03:49.843 --> 00:03:54.142
A governed directory, rationalized
before the move, not after.

63
00:03:54.142 --> 00:03:57.505
Continuous verification,
not one-time sign-in.

64
00:03:57.505 --> 00:04:01.317
Telemetry used for management,
not only incident response.

65
00:04:01.317 --> 00:04:05.956
And governance that runs automatically,
because manual review can't keep up.

66
00:04:05.956 --> 00:04:09.855
Continuous verification is
where old and new differ most.

67
00:04:09.855 --> 00:04:13.195
In Active Directory a user
signs in once and gets a

68
00:04:13.195 --> 00:04:16.780
ticket that lasts ten hours,
renewable for seven days.

69
00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:20.122
In Entra ID an access token
lasts one hour by default,

70
00:04:20.122 --> 00:04:24.319
and under continuous access
evaluation it can be revoked within

71
00:04:24.319 --> 00:04:28.205
about fifteen minutes of an
event such as a disabled account.

72
00:04:28.205 --> 00:04:30.368
Distance matters, but narrowly.

73
00:04:30.368 --> 00:04:34.027
It hurts the operations that
still need a domain controller,

74
00:04:34.027 --> 00:04:36.955
like sign-in and policy
on hybrid-joined devices.

75
00:04:36.955 --> 00:04:40.589
Light in fiber from Portland,
Oregon to Washington takes about

76
00:04:40.589 --> 00:04:44.643
forty-five milliseconds round trip,
and bandwidth doesn't change that.

77
00:04:44.643 --> 00:04:47.629
The durable answer is to
remove the dependency.

78
00:04:47.629 --> 00:04:51.080
Now, what changes, starting
with the platform.

79
00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:57.581
The platform is the one place where every

80
00:04:57.581 --> 00:05:00.817
decision is written down,
signed and replayable.

81
00:05:00.817 --> 00:05:03.922
The reference design runs every analysis,

82
00:05:03.922 --> 00:05:09.954
plan and evidence step on one Windows Server
2025 host, with a standby for recovery.

83
00:05:09.954 --> 00:05:14.893
What matters is a single authoritative
writer, because two writers would diverge.

84
00:05:14.893 --> 00:05:18.193
Every change is a signed
commit, never a portal click.

85
00:05:18.193 --> 00:05:20.030
Two rules matter at this level.

86
00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:23.405
First, governance and execution never mix.

87
00:05:23.405 --> 00:05:27.460
One layer validates and signs
plans but never touches a target;

88
00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:30.892
the other runs authorized
plans and decides nothing.

89
00:05:30.892 --> 00:05:33.098
They run under separate identities,

90
00:05:33.098 --> 00:05:37.217
because a component that both decides
and acts can cover its tracks.

91
00:05:37.217 --> 00:05:41.707
Second, the host reads the forest
and writes to cloud identity,

92
00:05:41.707 --> 00:05:43.380
so it's treated as Tier 0.

93
00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:47.696
The program's target posture for
federal production is gated actuation:

94
00:05:47.696 --> 00:05:51.868
the host proposes a correction, and
a person approves it before it runs.

95
00:05:51.868 --> 00:05:55.926
The decision belongs to the
program office, under the steward:

96
00:05:55.926 --> 00:05:59.269
stand up the host as the
only authoritative writer,

97
00:05:59.269 --> 00:06:03.805
protected as Tier 0, and record the
standby model and actuation stage.

98
00:06:03.805 --> 00:06:07.705
Gate G0 needs it operational
by the end of Phase 0.

99
00:06:07.705 --> 00:06:09.410
Next, identity.

100
00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:19.135
Identity comes first, because policy, devices
and access all target identity groups.

101
00:06:19.135 --> 00:06:24.797
The OU tree carried delegation, policy
scope and accountability in one structure.

102
00:06:24.797 --> 00:06:28.798
A flat cloud directory has none of
that until the program writes it down.

103
00:06:28.798 --> 00:06:32.184
The book rebuilds it with
attributes, dynamic groups,

104
00:06:32.184 --> 00:06:35.186
administrative units
and Conditional Access.

105
00:06:35.186 --> 00:06:40.263
Setting a user's attributes from the
HR record drives group membership,

106
00:06:40.263 --> 00:06:44.222
delegated scope, policy,
licenses and drift visibility.

107
00:06:44.222 --> 00:06:46.285
One stamp, five outcomes.

108
00:06:46.285 --> 00:06:48.910
That only works if the
attributes are true.

109
00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:53.435
For the workforce, the authoritative
source is the HR system of record.

110
00:06:53.435 --> 00:06:58.405
A dynamic rule is only as trustworthy as
whoever can write the attribute it reads,

111
00:06:58.405 --> 00:07:01.891
so the help-desk and self-service
write paths are removed,

112
00:07:01.891 --> 00:07:04.635
and HR provisioning
becomes the only writer.

113
00:07:04.635 --> 00:07:09.435
Tokens don't fix a wrong model; they
sign broken authorizations efficiently.

114
00:07:09.435 --> 00:07:12.751
So HR decides what the
authoritative attributes mean,

115
00:07:12.751 --> 00:07:15.698
and identity engineering
decides how they flow.

116
00:07:15.698 --> 00:07:18.722
Contractors need an authoritative
source of their own.

117
00:07:18.722 --> 00:07:23.110
Gate G1 requires the HR feed
working before any rollout.

118
00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:26.200
But people are only one
of four populations.

119
00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:34.701
Here's why this matters: "migrate
identity" hides four populations,

120
00:07:34.701 --> 00:07:38.925
and each moves by its own track,
with its own source of truth.

121
00:07:38.925 --> 00:07:44.775
People are the HR track, from HR or,
for contractors, the contractor source.

122
00:07:44.775 --> 00:07:47.186
End-user devices are the Intune track,

123
00:07:47.186 --> 00:07:51.075
with attributes written by device
provisioning, never by HR.

124
00:07:51.075 --> 00:07:55.975
Servers are the Arc track, tagged at
onboarding and governed by Azure Policy.

125
00:07:55.975 --> 00:08:00.128
And non-person identities, like
service accounts and AI agents,

126
00:08:00.128 --> 00:08:04.600
have no HR record; their owner and
sponsor are their accountability.

127
00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:08.658
The end state is the same for
all four: true attributes,

128
00:08:08.658 --> 00:08:13.362
one source each, read by policy,
with evidence for every change.

129
00:08:13.362 --> 00:08:16.800
Then AD can retire without
losing what the OU tree did.

130
00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:18.963
Group Policy splits the same way.

131
00:08:18.963 --> 00:08:20.580
Next, policy.

132
00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:29.239
Group Policy was Active Directory's most
ambitious delivery system, and its most

133
00:08:29.239 --> 00:08:33.855
network-bound: a client applies it only
when it can reach a domain controller.

134
00:08:33.855 --> 00:08:37.413
Its replacement is several
surfaces: Intune for devices,

135
00:08:37.413 --> 00:08:41.501
Conditional Access for access,
and Azure machine configuration

136
00:08:41.501 --> 00:08:45.892
through Arc for servers, because
Intune doesn't manage Windows Server.

137
00:08:45.892 --> 00:08:48.918
The program's pattern is
regenerate, not copy.

138
00:08:48.918 --> 00:08:51.745
Porting a fifty-setting
policy as fifty modern

139
00:08:51.745 --> 00:08:55.154
settings carries forward dead
weight and old conflicts.

140
00:08:55.154 --> 00:08:59.243
Microsoft's own recommendation is
to start new rather than migrate.

141
00:08:59.243 --> 00:09:01.604
A few rules govern every change.

142
00:09:01.604 --> 00:09:04.505
Each setting has exactly one owning layer.

143
00:09:04.505 --> 00:09:08.027
Require is preferred to block,
because a bad block locks

144
00:09:08.027 --> 00:09:11.167
people out and a bad require
adds a step you can fix.

145
00:09:11.167 --> 00:09:16.030
Every change goes through validation and
a report-only pilot before production.

146
00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:20.605
And the emergency access accounts are
excluded from every blocking policy.

147
00:09:20.605 --> 00:09:25.932
The decision is the CISO's: the control
requirements every surface must meet,

148
00:09:25.932 --> 00:09:30.593
meaning the Conditional Access
baseline, MFA strength and logging.

149
00:09:30.593 --> 00:09:34.881
Gate G1 needs that baseline
enforced, with no user outside

150
00:09:34.881 --> 00:09:39.617
the MFA requirement except the
documented emergency accounts.

151
00:09:39.617 --> 00:09:41.930
Next, network services.

152
00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:50.278
Network services are the part
organizations most often defer,

153
00:09:50.278 --> 00:09:54.080
because DNS, DHCP and address
management still work.

154
00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:57.218
That's exactly how they keep
the old directory alive.

155
00:09:57.218 --> 00:09:59.156
The three form one surface.

156
00:09:59.156 --> 00:10:03.653
DNS usually lives in zones
integrated with Active Directory,

157
00:10:03.653 --> 00:10:07.180
so Active Directory can't
retire until DNS moves.

158
00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:10.063
The forest's DHCP authorization stops an

159
00:10:10.063 --> 00:10:13.455
unauthorized Windows
server, not a rogue device.

160
00:10:13.455 --> 00:10:16.476
And address management,
in many organizations,

161
00:10:16.476 --> 00:10:18.955
is a spreadsheet one person maintains.

162
00:10:18.955 --> 00:10:22.255
Without it, DNS and DHCP drift.

163
00:10:22.255 --> 00:10:24.330
So the program moves them together.

164
00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:29.645
One governed address management authority
covers the whole address surface,

165
00:10:29.645 --> 00:10:34.142
on-premises and cloud, and DNS and
DHCP derive their records from it.

166
00:10:34.142 --> 00:10:36.468
Disagreement between them is drift.

167
00:10:36.468 --> 00:10:39.891
The common failure is to
modernize identity and policy

168
00:10:39.891 --> 00:10:43.167
and leave these for a later
phase that rarely happens.

169
00:10:43.167 --> 00:10:47.130
A forest that still hosts
DNS has not been retired.

170
00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:52.179
That makes it a scope and funding
decision for the sponsor and the CIO:

171
00:10:52.179 --> 00:10:57.493
put DNS, DHCP and IPAM in the core
program now, with one IPAM authority.

172
00:10:57.493 --> 00:11:00.405
The pilot division's
zones move in Phase 2.

173
00:11:00.405 --> 00:11:02.130
Next, compute.

174
00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:09.203
Once identity, policy and
network services have moved,

175
00:11:09.203 --> 00:11:13.042
domain-joined machines are the
largest remaining dependency.

176
00:11:13.042 --> 00:11:18.355
A machine leaves only when its identity,
policy and trust have each been replaced,

177
00:11:18.355 --> 00:11:21.843
so the domain join is the last
thing to go, not the first.

178
00:11:21.843 --> 00:11:25.755
User PCs end Entra joined
and managed in Intune.

179
00:11:25.755 --> 00:11:30.314
Hybrid join is a transition, and
there's no supported way to convert a

180
00:11:30.314 --> 00:11:35.605
hybrid-joined PC without resetting it, so
time that reset to a hardware refresh.

181
00:11:35.605 --> 00:11:40.712
Servers take a different road: Azure Arc
brings them under policy where they stand,

182
00:11:40.712 --> 00:11:44.167
and leaving the domain is
decided workload by workload.

183
00:11:44.167 --> 00:11:47.379
What really holds servers in
the domain is applications.

184
00:11:47.379 --> 00:11:52.780
Each Kerberos application is migrated
to modern authentication, retired, or,

185
00:11:52.780 --> 00:11:57.843
as a last resort, kept on a small
documented island with an end-of-life date.

186
00:11:57.843 --> 00:12:02.580
And an application's compute stays with
its data until its round trips are reduced.

187
00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:06.892
Only an application's owner can make
that call and sign off its cutover.

188
00:12:06.892 --> 00:12:12.679
Keeping legacy authentication open is
a risk acceptance, so the authorizing

189
00:12:12.679 --> 00:12:17.742
official sets every island and NTLM
end date, all resolved by Gate G4.

190
00:12:17.742 --> 00:12:19.400
Next, access.

191
00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:28.225
When Active Directory retires, being
inside the network stops meaning anything.

192
00:12:28.225 --> 00:12:33.363
M-22-09 says users should log in to
applications rather than networks,

193
00:12:33.363 --> 00:12:38.275
and multifactor authentication belongs
at the application layer, not in a VPN.

194
00:12:38.275 --> 00:12:40.982
The replacement is Zero
Trust as the model,

195
00:12:40.982 --> 00:12:45.623
Conditional Access deciding each
request, a secure access service edge

196
00:12:45.623 --> 00:12:50.187
replacing the VPN, and phishing-resistant
authentication as the proof.

197
00:12:50.187 --> 00:12:54.839
BOD 25-01 makes blocking
legacy authentication

198
00:12:54.839 --> 00:12:58.762
and phishing-resistant MFA
for all users mandatory.

199
00:12:58.762 --> 00:13:00.904
FIDO2 keys, passkeys,

200
00:13:00.904 --> 00:13:06.538
Windows Hello and PIV or CAC cards
qualify; SMS and voice don't.

201
00:13:06.538 --> 00:13:09.063
Two consequences need an executive eye.

202
00:13:09.063 --> 00:13:12.662
Smart-card sign-in is anchored
in Active Directory today.

203
00:13:12.662 --> 00:13:15.558
The card and PIN stay,
but certificate issuance

204
00:13:15.558 --> 00:13:18.750
needs a successor before
Active Directory retires.

205
00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:23.600
And privilege: every human admin assignment
becomes eligible and time-bound,

206
00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:27.162
and emergency accounts are
tested at least every 90 days.

207
00:13:27.162 --> 00:13:32.772
The CISO decides: phishing-resistant
MFA as the end state for everyone,

208
00:13:32.772 --> 00:13:36.480
Tier 0 administrators
first, opening in Phase 1,

209
00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:40.474
and certificate issuance
moved off AD DS by Gate G4.

210
00:13:40.474 --> 00:13:42.340
Next, who leads.

211
00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:47.977
The book's framing is simple.

212
00:13:47.977 --> 00:13:52.316
Microsoft provides the instruments;
the program provides the orchestra.

213
00:13:52.316 --> 00:13:54.464
Cloud identity, device management,

214
00:13:54.464 --> 00:13:58.465
Conditional Access and the
assessment tools are each excellent.

215
00:13:58.465 --> 00:14:00.515
But look at what each one doesn't decide.

216
00:14:00.515 --> 00:14:04.940
Cloud identity doesn't decide what your
organizational structure should become.

217
00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:08.365
The assessment tools don't decide
what to do with the findings.

218
00:14:08.365 --> 00:14:11.915
Every one of those gaps is a
decision someone has to own.

219
00:14:11.915 --> 00:14:16.610
And with no vendor deadline forcing
it, organizations without leadership

220
00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:21.153
migrate in pieces and end up unable to
answer last year's audit questions.

221
00:14:21.153 --> 00:14:26.290
The program needs no license of its own,
but the services it orchestrates do.

222
00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:30.678
Federal policy already says this
belongs to more than the IT shop.

223
00:14:30.678 --> 00:14:34.638
M-22-09 says the whole
C-suite must be aligned,

224
00:14:34.638 --> 00:14:39.103
and it required a zero trust
implementation lead within 30 days.

225
00:14:39.103 --> 00:14:45.331
M-19-17 requires an agency-wide identity
governance structure that includes HR,

226
00:14:45.331 --> 00:14:48.090
general counsel, privacy and acquisition.

227
00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:52.952
So the first decision for the sponsor
and the CIO, before Phase 0: confirm who

228
00:14:52.952 --> 00:14:58.127
already holds those roles, and decide
whether one becomes the program's steward.

229
00:14:58.127 --> 00:15:00.670
Next, the decision matrix.

230
00:15:04.420 --> 00:15:08.620
The book's decision matrix runs
on one rule: one decider per row.

231
00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:12.026
The single deliberate
exception is the pilot gate,

232
00:15:12.026 --> 00:15:14.945
G2, which the steward
and the sponsor co-sign.

233
00:15:14.945 --> 00:15:20.123
The sponsor and the CIO own scope, funding
and sequence, break ties, and decide

234
00:15:20.123 --> 00:15:25.795
when Active Directory components retire,
because each retirement ends a fallback.

235
00:15:25.795 --> 00:15:30.795
Their job is to keep this one program, not
five projects that finish one at a time.

236
00:15:30.795 --> 00:15:33.657
The CISO decides the control requirements.

237
00:15:33.657 --> 00:15:38.810
The authorizing official is the only one
who can accept risk on the agency's behalf,

238
00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:43.886
so every place the program carries risk
instead of removing it reaches that desk:

239
00:15:43.886 --> 00:15:46.537
a deferred legacy-authentication cutoff,

240
00:15:46.537 --> 00:15:51.083
a Kerberos island with no end date, a
deviation from a mandatory baseline.

241
00:15:51.083 --> 00:15:54.207
The steward's program
office runs the orchestra,

242
00:15:54.207 --> 00:15:56.394
but it doesn't decide risk or scope.

243
00:15:56.394 --> 00:15:59.509
Application owners decide
for their applications,

244
00:15:59.509 --> 00:16:02.333
and HR decides what the
worker attributes mean.

245
00:16:02.333 --> 00:16:07.349
The sponsor's decision, before the program
starts: fill in the matrix with names,

246
00:16:07.349 --> 00:16:10.127
one decider per row,
and get the authorizing

247
00:16:10.127 --> 00:16:13.369
official's agreement to
every risk-acceptance row.

248
00:16:13.369 --> 00:16:17.390
Next, three decisions that
often land on the wrong desk.

249
00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:24.065
Three decisions often
land on the wrong desk,

250
00:16:24.065 --> 00:16:26.515
and the book moves each one on purpose.

251
00:16:26.515 --> 00:16:30.778
First, end dates for legacy
authentication and NTLM.

252
00:16:30.778 --> 00:16:35.864
They sit with the authorizing official, not
with the engineers who'd like more time,

253
00:16:35.864 --> 00:16:39.977
because keeping legacy authentication
open is a risk acceptance,

254
00:16:39.977 --> 00:16:44.016
and BOD 25-01 makes blocking
it a mandatory baseline.

255
00:16:44.016 --> 00:16:47.540
Second, the help desk's
identity-verification procedure.

256
00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:52.053
The help desk supports users through
every wave, and that makes it a target.

257
00:16:52.053 --> 00:16:56.068
CISA's advisory on Scattered
Spider describes attackers

258
00:16:56.068 --> 00:16:59.839
phoning help desks to get
passwords reset and MFA moved.

259
00:16:59.839 --> 00:17:04.536
So the procedure for resets and temporary
access passes is a CISO decision,

260
00:17:04.536 --> 00:17:07.965
carried out by the help desk
with narrowly scoped roles.

261
00:17:07.965 --> 00:17:09.552
Third, records.

262
00:17:09.552 --> 00:17:13.652
Retiring Active Directory
retires years of access records.

263
00:17:13.652 --> 00:17:17.997
NARA's schedule keeps some for six
years after an account is terminated,

264
00:17:17.997 --> 00:17:21.827
and once a domain controller is
gone, its logs can't be recovered.

265
00:17:21.827 --> 00:17:26.640
So the records officer decides retention
before anything is decommissioned, and

266
00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:31.665
the privacy office is consulted before any
new attribute flow carries personal data.

267
00:17:31.665 --> 00:17:33.770
Now, how to deliver it.

268
00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:39.508
Delivery starts with the estate.

269
00:17:39.508 --> 00:17:43.031
You can't modernize what you haven't
enumerated, and nothing can be

270
00:17:43.031 --> 00:17:46.946
retired until someone has written
down everything it was quietly doing.

271
00:17:46.946 --> 00:17:51.867
A long-lived forest usually holds more
than anyone on the current team knows:

272
00:17:51.867 --> 00:17:55.851
unmapped trusts, Group Policy
whose authors left a decade ago,

273
00:17:55.851 --> 00:17:59.445
risky certificate templates,
and servers past support.

274
00:17:59.445 --> 00:18:01.695
The assessment follows three rules.

275
00:18:01.695 --> 00:18:06.182
It's read-only, enforced in the
account, the code and the audit trail.

276
00:18:06.182 --> 00:18:08.945
It's least-privilege,
never a domain admin.

277
00:18:08.945 --> 00:18:12.317
And every run carries
provenance: who ran it,

278
00:18:12.317 --> 00:18:15.408
when, against what, and with which rights.

279
00:18:15.408 --> 00:18:17.701
The output maps privileged accounts,

280
00:18:17.701 --> 00:18:20.733
so it's stored encrypted
and handled as sensitive.

281
00:18:20.733 --> 00:18:24.361
And because broad enumeration
looks like reconnaissance,

282
00:18:24.361 --> 00:18:26.508
security operations is told first.

283
00:18:26.508 --> 00:18:30.161
One more input decides whether
an application may move:

284
00:18:30.161 --> 00:18:34.370
how many round trips it makes to
its data, measured, not estimated.

285
00:18:34.370 --> 00:18:37.720
The steward commissions
the assessment in Phase 0.

286
00:18:37.720 --> 00:18:41.899
In the program's experience a first
pass takes days to a few weeks,

287
00:18:41.899 --> 00:18:43.870
and Gate G0 needs it committed.

288
00:18:43.870 --> 00:18:46.910
Next, how analysis becomes change.

289
00:18:50.660 --> 00:18:54.630
The rule here is simple: nothing
touches the target without a plan,

290
00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:58.085
and no plan touches it until
someone of record has read it.

291
00:18:58.085 --> 00:19:03.014
The cycle compares current state with the
target, then turns the difference into

292
00:19:03.014 --> 00:19:07.722
a plan of small actions, each with its own
inverse, so the plan can run backwards.

293
00:19:07.722 --> 00:19:10.942
Validation rejects plans
that break safety rules,

294
00:19:10.942 --> 00:19:13.548
such as deleting too many objects at once.

295
00:19:13.548 --> 00:19:17.336
Authorization needs at least one
approver who isn't the author.

296
00:19:17.336 --> 00:19:19.661
Only then does the execution layer,

297
00:19:19.661 --> 00:19:23.510
under a separate identity,
deliver and record each action.

298
00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:26.441
When something fails,
the default is to halt,

299
00:19:26.441 --> 00:19:29.535
and every run ends in a
signed evidence packet.

300
00:19:29.535 --> 00:19:32.470
A separate rule caps how
far automation may go,

301
00:19:32.470 --> 00:19:36.873
from simply recording up to autonomy
for named low-risk classes only.

302
00:19:36.873 --> 00:19:40.513
Tenant-wide role changes,
Conditional Access enforcement

303
00:19:40.513 --> 00:19:43.485
and bulk device changes
stay gated permanently.

304
00:19:43.485 --> 00:19:46.033
No federal document requires that ceiling;

305
00:19:46.033 --> 00:19:49.785
the book chooses it because an
authorizing official can sign it.

306
00:19:49.785 --> 00:19:54.654
So before the first production delivery,
the authorizing official agrees the

307
00:19:54.654 --> 00:19:59.672
ceiling for each class of change, with its
dry run, approver and rollback on record.

308
00:19:59.672 --> 00:20:01.610
Next, the roadmap.

309
00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:10.172
The roadmap sequences all of this as an
illustrative fifty-two-week program.

310
00:20:10.172 --> 00:20:13.310
The weeks are a sequencing
model, not a commitment;

311
00:20:13.310 --> 00:20:16.372
large federal estates
typically run multi-year.

312
00:20:16.372 --> 00:20:18.709
There are seven phases and six gates.

313
00:20:18.709 --> 00:20:22.185
Pre-flight stands up the platform
and runs the assessment.

314
00:20:22.185 --> 00:20:27.773
Foundation puts identity in place, including
the access baseline and the HR feed.

315
00:20:27.773 --> 00:20:31.663
A pilot division, typically
IT, proves the whole pattern,

316
00:20:31.663 --> 00:20:33.947
with zero drift for a hold period.

317
00:20:33.947 --> 00:20:37.485
Rollout is the longest
phase, division by division.

318
00:20:37.485 --> 00:20:41.181
Then legacy retirement,
then the shortest phase,

319
00:20:41.181 --> 00:20:44.785
retiring AD DS itself,
and finally steady state.

320
00:20:44.785 --> 00:20:46.422
Two properties define it.

321
00:20:46.422 --> 00:20:50.120
The five workstreams run in
parallel, each with a named lead,

322
00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:54.497
because finishing one before starting
the next doubles the duration.

323
00:20:54.497 --> 00:20:58.798
And it's gated: a phase opens only
when the previous gate clears.

324
00:20:58.798 --> 00:21:01.685
The decision closes the loop on ownership.

325
00:21:01.685 --> 00:21:04.240
Put the gate dates on
the sponsor's calendar,

326
00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:08.073
and the end date of every exception
on the authorizing official's.

327
00:21:08.073 --> 00:21:11.223
Gate G0 gets its date in Phase 0.

328
00:21:11.223 --> 00:21:14.110
Next, what makes the gates trustworthy.

329
00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:19.960
Two things make the gates trustworthy.

330
00:21:19.960 --> 00:21:23.184
First, every gate criterion
is evaluated from the

331
00:21:23.184 --> 00:21:26.723
repository and the drift
reports, not from a signature.

332
00:21:26.723 --> 00:21:31.822
Zero drift for fourteen days is a
query; so is every PC cloud-joined.

333
00:21:31.822 --> 00:21:35.222
A gate the engine can't evaluate
needs a better criterion.

334
00:21:35.222 --> 00:21:38.147
Second, rollback triggers are thresholds,

335
00:21:38.147 --> 00:21:41.573
not judgment calls, and
the agency sets the values.

336
00:21:41.573 --> 00:21:44.272
Drift above the threshold halts the phase.

337
00:21:44.272 --> 00:21:47.530
Conditional Access blocking
too many legitimate

338
00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:49.934
sign-ins means the policy is reverted.

339
00:21:49.934 --> 00:21:53.760
If drift detection stops
reporting, all phase work halts.

340
00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:55.832
Authority is fixed in advance,

341
00:21:55.832 --> 00:22:00.473
from the steward on the early gates
to the sponsor and CIO on the last.

342
00:22:00.473 --> 00:22:02.060
And be clear about the end.

343
00:22:02.060 --> 00:22:05.367
A forest in backup isn't
a lasting fallback:

344
00:22:05.367 --> 00:22:11.035
a backup is valid only within the
tombstone lifetime, 180 days by default.

345
00:22:11.035 --> 00:22:15.522
The real rollback point is the last
moment a domain controller still runs.

346
00:22:15.522 --> 00:22:19.523
So G5 is the sponsor's and the
CIO's decision, made on evidence:

347
00:22:19.523 --> 00:22:24.178
no live production dependency on
Active Directory authentication,

348
00:22:24.178 --> 00:22:27.934
or a documented Kerberos island
with an end-of-life date.

349
00:22:27.934 --> 00:22:30.420
Next, the external clocks.

350
00:22:34.170 --> 00:22:38.345
Two external clocks bear on the
schedule, and they're easy to confuse.

351
00:22:38.345 --> 00:22:43.414
FedRAMP's Consolidated Rules
for 2026, announced on June 25,

352
00:22:43.414 --> 00:22:47.345
2026, govern how cloud
services are certified.

353
00:22:47.345 --> 00:22:51.018
They become mandatory on January 1, 2027,

354
00:22:51.018 --> 00:22:56.695
and new Rev5 certification
applications stop on June 11, 2027.

355
00:22:56.695 --> 00:22:59.595
None of that certifies
the agency's own estate.

356
00:22:59.595 --> 00:23:02.777
Each agency still
authorizes its own systems;

357
00:23:02.777 --> 00:23:08.470
what the 2027 date changes is the evidence
your providers publish and you inherit.

358
00:23:08.470 --> 00:23:15.017
The deadlines that bind the agency
directly come from OMB and CISA: M-26-14's

359
00:23:15.017 --> 00:23:17.973
logging plan and maturity levels,

360
00:23:17.973 --> 00:23:23.570
and BOD 25-01's baselines
for Microsoft 365 tenants.

361
00:23:23.570 --> 00:23:25.620
The estate has its own dates too.

362
00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:30.598
Windows 10 reached end of
support on October 14, 2025,

363
00:23:30.598 --> 00:23:36.618
Windows Server 2012 security
updates ended on October 13, 2026,

364
00:23:36.618 --> 00:23:42.870
and Windows Server 2016 reaches
end of support on January 12, 2027.

365
00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:48.170
Mainframes join the access work late,
and may need other zero trust mechanisms.

366
00:23:48.170 --> 00:23:53.109
So the sponsor and the CIO sequence
the program against the agency's own

367
00:23:53.109 --> 00:23:58.633
deadlines and end-of-support dates, starting
now, not against FedRAMP's 2027 date.

368
00:23:58.633 --> 00:24:01.660
To close, the decisions in one place.

369
00:24:05.410 --> 00:24:08.110
So, the decisions, with their owners.

370
00:24:08.110 --> 00:24:12.269
Agency leadership and the CIO name
the sponsor, the steward and the

371
00:24:12.269 --> 00:24:17.260
authorizing official, and the sponsor
fills the matrix with one decider per row.

372
00:24:17.260 --> 00:24:22.727
The sponsor and the CIO scope all eleven
categories, with DNS, DHCP and address

373
00:24:22.727 --> 00:24:28.535
management in the core program, and at the
end retire Active Directory on evidence.

374
00:24:28.535 --> 00:24:31.415
The steward stands up
the platform host and

375
00:24:31.415 --> 00:24:34.935
commissions the read-only
assessment, both in Phase 0.

376
00:24:34.935 --> 00:24:39.097
The CISO sets the control
requirements, enforced at Gate G1.

377
00:24:39.097 --> 00:24:41.860
HR defines what the
worker attributes mean.

378
00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:44.872
The authorizing official
agrees the automation

379
00:24:44.872 --> 00:24:47.222
ceiling and every exception's end date.

380
00:24:47.222 --> 00:24:51.772
And the records officer settles retention
before anything is decommissioned.

381
00:24:51.772 --> 00:24:54.411
None of this needs a vendor
decision on day one.

382
00:24:54.411 --> 00:24:59.094
It needs an inventory, an owner and
a date: the read-only assessment,

383
00:24:59.094 --> 00:25:02.998
a named sponsor and steward,
and Gate G0 on the calendar.

384
00:25:02.998 --> 00:25:05.473
Microsoft has supplied the instruments.

385
00:25:05.473 --> 00:25:10.097
The program is the orchestra, and the people
named in this briefing have to play it.

386
00:25:10.097 --> 00:25:13.838
Retiring Active Directory is
a governance transformation,

387
00:25:13.838 --> 00:25:15.409
and it should be led as one.

388
00:25:15.409 --> 00:25:19.485
Book 01 has the detail,
with every claim footnoted.

389
00:25:19.485 --> 00:25:20.310
Thank you.
